course: dictionary
lesson: 157
title: One small word, many jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: «After» is a small word with several jobs. Most often it means later in time: «Lunch comes after breakfast» tells you the order, so breakfast happens first and lunch second.
explain: It can also mean behind or following. In «The dog ran after the ball», the dog was chasing the ball. Ask yourself: is the sentence about time, chasing, or caring? That question will usually point you to the right meaning.
explain: Some phrases change the meaning. To look after someone means to take care of them. In «Lily stays with Chloe in the nurse's room after Chloe's plaits are pulled so hard she cries», after means later in time: first the plaits are pulled, then Lily stays.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in a simple word such as "later" or "behind". If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right one.
fact: After means later than something else when it is about time.
fact: «Pentecostal churches are named after Pentecost» means they take their name from Pentecost.
fact: «Saving means putting money aside, week after week» means one week following another, again and again.
fact: In Chinese, «xiàwǔ» means afternoon, literally "after noon".
fact: In Irish English, «I'm after eating» means I have just eaten, and in Australia «arvo» means afternoon.
exercise: «Lunch comes after breakfast.» Which meal is first? | They happen at the same time | Lunch | Neither | *Breakfast
exercise: «The dog ran after the ball.» What does after mean here? | *Chasing, going along behind | Looking at | Before | Later in time
exercise: Mum asks Sam to look after his little sister. What does look after mean? | *Take care of | Walk in front of | Forget about | Wait for
test: «Pentecostal churches are named after Pentecost.» In this sentence, named after means | Are bigger than | Are the same as Pentecost | *Take their name from | Are older than
test: Which word means the same as after in a formal exam? | Yesterday | Previously | *Subsequently | Beforehand
exam: Complete the Irish English sentence: "I'm ___ eating" (meaning I have just eaten). | before | over | *after | since
